 people to trust me in  the city  i didn't mean to make those girls the  the subject of ridicule i  honestly didn't  but that's what had happened  and um she would actually go out to  interviews like there was um there was a  church that was starting and it had been  a crack house and i was trying to get  the neighbors to open up to me about it  because i wanted to do a story on  like that's a pretty cool story a crack  house turning into a church and joanne  went and sat on the porch of every  neighborhood every neighbor in the  around that and she vouched for me and  so she did the same thing much  years later for me with this too  and um  again it was  uh  they had read my stories they had they  had seen the work i had done since then  where i wasn't just doing like  drive-by anecdote reporting i was really  digging in spending time with people and  so i think i was able to sort of reap  the bounty of just my time there  well one low point in your research you  complained to canadian historian jane  nicholas about the difficulties of your  task and she gave you a valuable piece  of advice saying  if we only wrote the histories of the  people who left detailed records we  would only get to know about the really  privileged people  you have to piece together your evidence  with empathy and conjecture using the  materials at hand how did her words  inspire you well i was just beating  myself up because there were so many  holes still  and um  i thought well that's right you know  nobody the way she put it like that it  it gave me permission  that maybe my story wasn't perfect i  didn't have every single year every  place they were but there was a reason i  didn't have that and that was because of  this institutional racism and the  coverage of it and it just  if i can write about that then i can  cast it into even more deeper context  and that really helped me  um i also complained once to one of  nancy's younger relatives that  it was really hard to get some of the