 about connecting the past and  the present and this is your advice for  young reporters get away from your damn  smartphones and computers  go back to the basics papers scissors  real people be the glue as a great  reporter mary bishop once told you  connecting stranger to stranger if only  for an instant can you elaborate on how  this has just been your your mo  well i mean documents can only take you  so far memoirs written i mean that was a  great find when they found the guy  bragging about buying them all the  paperwork can take you so far but  there's no substitute for um going and  meeting people and hearing their stories  i mean the best parts of the book i  think are this  this these um gritty kind of  microaggressions that these men and  women lived with during jim crow like  the little girls who were walking to  school pastel white lady's house and  there's two parrots on the side porch  and the parents have been trained to  squawk racial epithets at them and this  is what these are the memories they have  these ladies in their mid-80s  rent collectors who accepted sex and  partial payment  um i would have never had these stories  had i not gone out spent the time to get  to know people and them to know me and  to have them open up to stories with  stories and soul of your book well thank  you in addition to the remarkable story  one thing also if you could comment on  was because the brothers were portrayed  in the circus sort of as being imbeciles  can you talk about  willie's intelligence yeah that you yeah  it turns out a friend of mine who's a  lawyer just happened to have deposed him  for that burn lawsuit nick leach  and uh  so i took him out to lunch i was having  trouble getting those  final documents i mean that was quite  like they were misfiled it had the  circuit court clerk had to help me with  that and so i took him out to lunch he  was giving me advice on how i could find  those and he says oh i deposed him once  i said well what was he l